<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" modified="2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z" agent="Trae"><diagram id="pipeline" name="Pipeline"><mxGraphModel dx="1800" dy="2600" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2200" pageHeight="3200" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" /><mxCell id="header" value="SciAtlas Idea Generation Pipeline&#10;Run dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_question/03-&#10;微观界面测量/full&#10;Topic: Research question: How can we measure interface&#10;phenomena on the microscopic level?&#10;Context: Interfacial chemistry is a fascinating field that&#10;seeks to expand and deepen our comprehension of behavior&#10;at the molecular interface of gases and liquids, liquids&#10;and solids, and solids and solids, among other systems. At&#10;these microscopic and nanoscale borders, an elemental&#10;dance occurs involving mass and heat transfer through&#10;evaporation, reaction, and micro- or even nanoscale&#10;explosions, but we still are unsure how these processes&#10;are choreographed by the chemicals themselves. In the case&#10;of gas–liquid or liquid–solid interfaces, optical&#10;interference methods can measure gas-film or liquid-film&#10;thicknesses up to nanoscale levels, but the information is&#10;too limited for sharpening our understanding of these&#10;complex physical and chemical phenomena. The study of&#10;microscopic interfaces is fueled by urgent research needs&#10;in fields such as materials synthesis, combustion, spray&#10;drying, and design of next-generation evaporative cooling&#10;nanodevices.&#10;Status: failed&#10;Failed step: step9" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="40" width="442" height="546" /></mxCell><mxCell id="params" value="Input Parameters&#10;topic: Research question: How can we measure interface&#10;phenomena on the microscopic level?&#10;Context: Interfacial chemistry is a fascinating field that&#10;seeks to expand and deepen our comprehension of behavior&#10;at the molecular interface of gases and liquids, liquids&#10;and solids, and solids and solids, among other systems. At&#10;these microscopic and nanoscale borders, an elemental&#10;dance occurs involving mass and heat transfer through&#10;evaporation, reaction, and micro- or even nanoscale&#10;explosions, but we still are unsure how these processes&#10;are choreographed by the chemicals themselves. In the case&#10;of gas–liquid or liquid–solid interfaces, optical&#10;interference methods can measure gas-film or liquid-film&#10;thicknesses up to nanoscale levels, but the information is&#10;too limited for sharpening our understanding of these&#10;complex physical and chemical phenomena. The study of&#10;microscopic interfaces is fueled by urgent research needs&#10;in fields such as materials synthesis, combustion, spray&#10;drying, and design of next-generation evaporative cooling&#10;nanodevices.&#10;domain: Chemistry&#10;resume_from_run_dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_q&#10;uestion/03-微观界面测量/full&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;&#10;Effective Pipeline Config&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;anchor_top_k: 5&#10;keyword_min_papers: 2&#10;k_step1: 4&#10;seed_num_queries: 8&#10;seed_per_query: 3&#10;seed_request_multiplier: 6&#10;seed_request_floor: 12&#10;seed_recent_years: 7&#10;seed_min_citations: 1000&#10;graph_budget_ratio: 0.35&#10;graph_budget_min: 12&#10;graph_budget_max: 30&#10;graph_max_predecessors_per_paper: 3&#10;graph_min_forward_papers: 3&#10;graph_candidate_fetch_limit: 40&#10;num_candidate_step2: 3&#10;num_expansion_step2: 1&#10;innoeval_root: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/SciAtlas/referen&#10;ces/search/src/innoeval_search&#10;grobid_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8070&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_same_field: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;max_novelty_feedback_rounds: 2&#10;idea_count: 1&#10;runs_dir: runs&#10;&#10;Effective SciAtlas Config&#10;base_url: http://sciatlas.openkg.cn&#10;api_key: ***REDACTED***&#10;timeout: 900&#10;max_retries: 4&#10;retry_backoff: 5.0&#10;use_cache: True&#10;cache_dir: runs/cache&#10;official_cli_root: .&#10;use_local_kg: False&#10;local_search_root: references/search" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="40" width="442" height="1320" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step1" value="Step 1 Anchor Retrieval&#10;status: resumed&#10;seed_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="340" width="220" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step2" value="Step 2 Research Graph Construction&#10;status: resumed&#10;graph_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="500" width="274" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step1_step_step2" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step1" target="step_step2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step2_graph_title" value="Step 2 Annotated Research Graph&#10;paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0&#10;phase_count: 1&#10;directions: anchor=4, backward=0,&#10;forward=0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=13;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="500" width="267" height="132" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_title" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step2_graph_title"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed" value="Seed Paper 1/4&#10;Nanoscale Mapping of the&#10;Directional Flow Patterns at&#10;Liquid-Solid Interfaces&#10;year: 2020&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W3028689404&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_0" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_1" value="Seed Paper 2/4&#10;Nanoscale Electron Transfer&#10;Variations at&#10;Electrocatalyst–Electrolyte&#10;Interfaces Resolved by &lt;i&gt;in&#10;Situ&lt;/i&gt; Conductive Atomic Force&#10;Microscopy&#10;year: 2023&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4321480595&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="640" y="672" width="260" height="212" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_1" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_2" value="Seed Paper 3/4&#10;Interfacial wettability and mass&#10;transfer characterizations for&#10;gas–liquid–solid triple‐phase&#10;catalysis&#10;year: 2022&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4223928347&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_2" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_3" value="Seed Paper 4/4&#10;A mobile setup for simultaneous&#10;and &lt;i&gt;in situ&lt;/i&gt; neutron&#10;reflectivity, infrared&#10;spectroscopy, and ellipsometry&#10;studies&#10;year: 2022&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4308711154&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1200" y="672" width="260" height="196" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_3" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: resumed&#10;serialized_chars: 6550&#10;trend_chars: 8466" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="984" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: How can we measure&#10;interface phenomena on the microscopic level? Context:&#10;Interfacial chemistry is a fascinating field that seeks to&#10;expand and deepen our comprehension of behavior at the&#10;molecular interface of gases and liquids, liquids and solids,&#10;and solids and solids, among other systems. At these&#10;microscopic and nanoscale borders, an elemental dance occurs&#10;involving mass and heat transfer through evaporation,&#10;reaction, and micro- or even nanoscale explosions, but we&#10;still are unsure how these processes are choreographed by the&#10;chemicals themselves. In the case of gas–liquid or&#10;liquid–solid interfaces, optical interference methods can&#10;measure gas-film or liquid-film thicknesses up to nanoscale&#10;levels, but the information is too limited for sharpening our&#10;understanding of these complex physical and chemical&#10;phenomena. The study of microscopic interfaces is fueled by&#10;urgent research needs in fields such as materials synthesis,&#10;combustion, spray drying, and design of next-generation&#10;evaporative cooling nanodevices. seed_paper: Nanoscale Mapping&#10;of the Directional Flow Patterns at Liquid-Solid Interfaces #&#10;CITATION SUBGRAPH (3 refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1]&#10;Nanoscale Electron Transfer Variations at&#10;Electrocatalyst–Electrolyte Interfaces Resolved by &lt;i&gt;in&#10;Situ&lt;/i&gt; Conductive Atomic Force Microscopy (2023, venue=N/A)&#10;authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth = 1 cited_in_sections = []&#10;cite_count = 48 section_weight = 0.0 delta_year = unknown&#10;is_influential = False low_confidence = True cited_by_subgraph&#10;= 0 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[] delta_yr=unknown&#10;edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: Rational innovation of&#10;electrocatalysts requires detailed knowledge of spatial&#10;property variations across the solid-electrolyte interface. We&#10;introduce correlative atomic force microscopy (AFM) to&#10;simultaneously probe, &lt;i&gt;in situ&lt;/i&gt; and at the nanoscale,&#10;electrical conductivity, chemical-frictional Existing Methods:&#10;Unavailable from local metadata. Motivation: Rational&#10;innovation of electrocatalysts requires detailed knowledge of&#10;spatial property variations across the solid-electrolyte&#10;interface. We introduce correlative atomic force microscopy&#10;(AFM) to simultaneously probe, &lt;i&gt;in situ&lt;/i&gt; and at the&#10;nanoscale, electrical conductivity, chemical-frictional&#10;Proposed Method: Rational innovation of electrocatalysts&#10;requires detailed knowledge of spatial property variations&#10;across the solid-electrolyte interface. We introduce&#10;correlative atomic force microscopy (AFM) to simultaneously&#10;probe, &lt;i&gt;in situ&lt;/i&gt; and at the nanoscale, electrical&#10;conductivity, chemical-frictional Experiment Plan: Unavailable&#10;from local metadata. [ABSTRACT] Rational innovation of&#10;electrocatalysts requires detailed knowledge of spatial&#10;property variations across the solid-electrolyte interface. We&#10;introduce correlative atomic force microscopy (AFM) to&#10;simultaneously probe, &lt;i&gt;in situ&lt;/i&gt; and at the nanoscale,&#10;electrical conductivity, chemical-frictional, and&#10;morphologic... ## [2] Interfacial wettability and mass&#10;transfer characterizations for gas–liquid–solid triple‐phase&#10;catalysis (2022, venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth =&#10;1 cited_in_sections = []...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: Paper 0 to Paper 1: Interpreting Paper 0 as the 2022&#10;mobile neutron reflectivity / infrared spectroscopy /&#10;ellipsometry study and Paper 1 as the 2022 gas–liquid–solid&#10;triple-phase catalysis study, the field moves from non-&#10;destructive structural profiling of buried solid/liquid&#10;interfaces toward functional characterization of multiphase&#10;reactive environments. The neutron reflectivity work&#10;emphasizes molecular-length-scale resolution of thickness,&#10;density, roughness, and layered structure, while adding&#10;infrared and ellipsometric information in a simultaneous in&#10;situ configuration. This addresses a central limitation of&#10;simple optical interference methods: thickness alone is&#10;insufficient to explain interfacial chemistry. The transition&#10;to triple-phase catalysis expands the measurement problem from&#10;relatively controlled solid/liquid layered structures to&#10;chemically active gas–liquid–solid systems where wettability,&#10;phase contact, and mass transfer directly determine catalytic&#10;performance. The key advance is therefore conceptual:&#10;interfacial measurement is no longer only about resolving&#10;static film geometry, but about connecting interfacial&#10;structure to transport and reaction in realistic multiphase&#10;systems. Paper 1 to Paper 2: Moving from Paper 1, the 2022&#10;triple-phase wettability/mass-transfer characterization, to&#10;Paper 2, the 2023 in situ conductive atomic force microscopy&#10;study of electrocatalyst–electrolyte interfaces, the research&#10;direction shifts from mesoscale descriptions of phase contact&#10;and mass transfer toward nanoscale operando mapping of local&#10;interfacial function. The triple-phase work highlights that&#10;catalytic behavior depends on how gas, liquid, and solid&#10;phases meet and how reactants move through the interfacial&#10;microenvironment, but it does not fully resolve local&#10;electron-transfer heterogeneity at the nanometer scale. The&#10;2023 AFM-based work advances this by introducing correlative&#10;in situ probing of electrical conductivity,&#10;chemical/frictional response, and morphology at the&#10;solid–electrolyte boundary. This represents a shift from&#10;ensemble or averaged interfacial descriptors to spatially&#10;resolved local measurements of electron transfer and surface&#10;property variation. In the context of the broader topic, this&#10;is a major step toward measuring microscopic interface&#10;phenomena directly under working conditions rather than&#10;inferring them..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="984" width="560" height="1986" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: resumed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3070" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Microscopic and nanoscale interfacial chemistry&#10;and physics of gas-liquid, liquid-solid, gas-liquid-solid, and&#10;solid-solid interfaces, with emphasis on measuring coupled&#10;structure, transport, reaction, and thermal phenomena under&#10;operating conditions.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Existing interfacial measurement approaches provide&#10;only partial, modality-specific observables and cannot&#10;simultaneously resolve coupled nanoscale structure, chemical&#10;state, charge transfer, flow, temperature, and mass/heat flux&#10;at operating multiphase interfaces, nor quantitatively connect&#10;this local heterogeneity to macroscopic evaporation, reaction,&#10;combustion, spray-drying, or cooling rates.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- gas-liquid interface&#10;- liquid-solid interface&#10;- gas-liquid-solid triple-phase interface&#10;- solid-solid interface&#10;- nanoscale interfacial film&#10;- film thickness&#10;- density and roughness of interfacial layers&#10;- molecular layering&#10;- wettability&#10;- mass transfer&#10;- heat transfer&#10;- evaporation&#10;- interfacial reaction&#10;- electron transfer&#10;- local conductivity&#10;- chemical-frictional response&#10;- interfacial morphology&#10;- directional flow patterns&#10;- local concentration gradients&#10;- transient interfacial events&#10;- film rupture&#10;- bubble nucleation&#10;- local evaporation bursts&#10;- microscale or nanoscale explosions&#10;- operando interfacial conditions&#10;- macroscopic device performance&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- Interfacial structure and chemistry determine wettability,&#10;mass transfer, heat transfer, and reaction pathways.&#10;- Local nanoscale heterogeneity controls electron transfer,&#10;reactive hot spots, and chemical-frictional behavior.&#10;- Triple-phase contact and interfacial microenvironment govern&#10;reactant access and catalytic performance.&#10;- Film thickness, density, roughness, and molecular layering&#10;modulate transport across interfaces.&#10;- Directional flow and local concentration gradients couple to&#10;evaporation, heat removal, combustion, spray drying, and&#10;materials synthesis.&#10;- Multimodal interfacial observables must be integrated to&#10;infer absolute local fluxes and reaction rates.&#10;- Nanoscale interfacial events aggregate into macroscopic&#10;transport, reaction, and device performance.&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- Single measurement techniques provide partial observables&#10;rather than a complete coupled interfacial state.&#10;- Reflectivity and ellipsometry can resolve structural&#10;layering but do not directly capture local electron-transfer&#10;heterogeneity.&#10;- Atomic force microscopy can map local electrical, chemical-&#10;frictional, and morphological properties but may miss bulk&#10;liquid structure, buried interfaces, and long-range transport&#10;fields.&#10;- Triple-phase wettability and mass-transfer characterization&#10;captures functional transport context but may lack nanoscale&#10;mechanistic resolution.&#10;- Buried, optically complex, geometrically confined, or&#10;chemically reactive interfaces are difficult to access without&#10;perturbing the phenomena.&#10;- Transient events such as film rupture, bubble nucleation,&#10;local evaporation bursts, and rapid reactions are difficult to&#10;observe with sufficient spatiotemporal resolution and chemical&#10;specificity.&#10;- There is no robust framework for converting multimodal&#10;interfacial signals into absolute local fluxes, reaction&#10;rates, or transport coefficients.&#10;- Directional interfacial flow and local concentration&#10;gradients near microscopic interfaces remain difficult to&#10;measure directly.&#10;- Local nanoscale measurements are not yet reliably scalable&#10;to reactor-, coating-, catalyst-, or device-level performance&#10;predictions.&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- Spatial resolution versus temporal resolution versus&#10;chemical specificity.&#10;- Surface sensitivity versus non-destructive access to buried&#10;interfaces.&#10;- Operando realism versus measurement perturbation.&#10;- Local nanoscale mapping versus large-area transport-field&#10;observation.&#10;- Multimodal completeness versus signal interpretability and&#10;quantitative integration.&#10;- Ensemble-averaged structural descriptors versus&#10;heterogeneous local mechanisms.&#10;- Direct local measurement versus inference of macroscopic&#10;rates and performance.&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- Simultaneous or tightly correlative measurement of&#10;interfacial structure, chemistry, charge transfer, flow,&#10;temperature, and mass/heat transfer.&#10;- Nanoscale spatial resolution with sufficient temporal&#10;resolution to capture transient interfacial events.&#10;- Operando, non-destructive access to gas-liquid, liquid-&#10;solid, gas-liquid-solid, and buried solid-solid interfaces.&#10;- Quantitative inversion from multimodal signals to absolute&#10;local fluxes, reaction rates, and transport coefficients.&#10;- Multiscale correlation from molecular or nanoscale&#10;observations to device-scale performance.&#10;- Robust operation under realistic pressure, temperature,&#10;electrochemical, catalytic, evaporative, or multiphase&#10;conditions.&#10;- Minimal perturbation of the interface being measured.&#10;- Ability to connect local heterogeneity to evaporation,&#10;combustion, spray drying, materials synthesis, or evaporative&#10;cooling behavior.&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- Correlative multimodal in situ or operando sensing combining&#10;structural, spectroscopic, electrical, thermal, and mechanical&#10;contrast.&#10;- Physics-informed inversion and data fusion to convert&#10;heterogeneous interfacial observables into local fluxes and&#10;reaction rates.&#10;- Multiscale transport-reaction modeling linking nanoscale&#10;heterogeneity to macroscopic performance.&#10;- Contrast- or tracer-based mechanisms for measuring mass&#10;flow, heat flow, and concentration fields near interfaces.&#10;- Scanning-probe mechanisms for local conductivity, chemical-&#10;frictional response, and morphology under working conditions.&#10;- Integrated reflectivity, spectroscopy, and ellipsometry&#10;mechanisms for molecular-length-scale structural profiling.&#10;- Multiphase microenvironment measurement and control&#10;mechanisms for triple-phase catalysis, evaporation, and&#10;reactive transport.&#10;- Digital-twin or statistical upscaling frameworks that&#10;reconcile local nanoscale measurements with device-scale&#10;behavior.&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- Operando electrochemical and catalytic microscopy for local&#10;charge transfer and chemical heterogeneity.&#10;- Multiphase microfluidics and evaporative cooling devices for&#10;local flow, heat, and mass transfer measurement.&#10;- Neutron, X-ray, reflectivity, scattering, and spectroscopic&#10;methods for buried soft-matter and liquid interfaces.&#10;- Scanning thermal microscopy, quantitative phase imaging, and&#10;tracer velocimetry for temperature, film morphology, and&#10;directional flow.&#10;- Combustion, spray drying, and materials synthesis&#10;diagnostics for transient interfacial events.&#10;- Physics-informed inverse transport models and digital twins&#10;for scaling local interfacial measurements to device&#10;performance." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3070" width="560" height="3174" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: resumed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6344" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: Same-field retrieval is needed to ground solutions&#10;in operando interfacial measurement, multimodal&#10;characterization, and triple-phase transport-reaction science.&#10;Cross-domain retrieval is also justified because key&#10;bottlenecks require mechanism transfer, including physics-&#10;informed inversion, multiscale digital-twin upscaling,&#10;thermal/flow diagnostics, and non-destructive buried-interface&#10;sensing." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6344" width="560" height="330" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;candidate_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6774" width="239" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Surface and interfacial sciences for future&#10;technologies&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Physical science has undergone an evolutional&#10;transition in research focus from solid bulks to surfaces,&#10;culminating in numerous prominent achievements. Currently, it&#10;is experiencing a new exploratory phase-interfacial science.&#10;Many a technology with a tremendous impact is clo...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;d